content:
Below is a simple X++ example for creating a custom REST-style service in Dynamics AX / Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations.

> Note: In Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations, custom services are exposed as REST endpoints under:
>
> ```text
> /api/services/{ServiceGroupName}/{ServiceName}/{OperationName}
> ```
>
> In AX 2012, custom services are normally exposed through AIF inbound ports, usually SOAP-based, unless additional configuration/customization is done.

---

## 1. Create a Data Contract Class

This class represents the request coming into the service.

```x++
[DataContractAttribute]
class CustomerRequestContract
{
    CustAccount custAccount;
}

[DataMemberAttribute("CustAccount")]
public CustAccount parmCustAccount(CustAccount _custAccount = custAccount)
{
    custAccount = _custAccount;
    return custAccount;
}
```

---

## 2. Create a Response Contract Class

This class represents the response returned by the service.

```x++
[DataContractAttribute]
class CustomerResponseContract
{
    CustAccount custAccount;
    Name        customerName;
    boolean     success;
    str         message;
}

[DataMemberAttribute("CustAccount")]
public CustAccount parmCustAccount(CustAccount _custAccount = custAccount)
{
    custAccount = _custAccount;
    return custAccount;
}

[DataMemberAttribute("CustomerName")]
public Name parmCustomerName(Name _customerName = customerName)
{
    customerName = _customerName;
    return customerName;
}

[DataMemberAttribute("Success")]
public boolean parmSuccess(boolean _success = success)
{
    success = _success;
    return success;
}

[DataMemberAttribute("Message")]
public str parmMessage(str _message = message)
{
    message = _message;
    return message;
}
```

---

## 3. Create the Service Class

This class contains the actual service method.

```x++
class CustomerRestService
{
}

[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
public CustomerResponseContract getCustomer(CustomerRequestContract _request)
{
    CustTable                custTable;
    CustomerResponseContract response;

    response = new CustomerResponseContract();

    if (!_request || !_request.parmCustAccount())
    {
        response.parmSuccess(false);
        response.parmMessage("Customer account is required.");
        return response;
    }

    select firstOnly custTable
        where custTable.AccountNum == _request.parmCustAccount();

    if (custTable)
    {
        response.parmSuccess(true);
        response.parmCustAccount(custTable.AccountNum);
        response.parmCustomerName(custTable.name());
        response.parmMessage("Customer found.");
    }
    else
    {
        response.parmSuccess(false);
        response.parmCustAccount(_request.parmCustAccount());
        response.parmMessage("Customer not found.");
    }

    return response;
}
```

---

## 4. Create a Service in the AOT

In Visual Studio or AOT:

1. Create a new **Service**.
2. Name it, for example:

```text
CustomerRestService
```

3. Add the class `CustomerRestService` as the service class.
4. Add the operation:

```text
getCustomer
```

---

## 5. Create a Service Group

Create a new **Service Group** and add the service to it.

Example:

```text
CustomerRestServiceGroup
```

Add:

```text
CustomerRestService
```

Then deploy the service group.

---

## 6. REST Endpoint URL

After deployment, the service can be called using a URL like this:

```text
https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/CustomerRestServiceGroup/CustomerRestService/getCustomer
```

---

## 7. Example JSON Request

Call the service using `POST`.

```json
{
    "_request": {
        "CustAccount": "US-001"
    }
}
```

---

## 8. Example JSON Response

```json
{
    "CustAccount": "US-001",
    "CustomerName": "Contoso Retail",
    "Success": true,
    "Message": "Customer found."
}
```

---

## 9. Example Postman Setup

Use the following settings in Postman:

```text
Method: POST
URL: https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/CustomerRestServiceGroup/CustomerRestService/getCustomer
Authorization: Bearer token
Content-Type: application/json
```

Body:

```json
{
    "_request": {
        "CustAccount": "US-001"
    }
}
```

---

## Important Notes

- The service method must be marked with:

```x++
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
```

- Contract classes should use:

```x++
[DataContractAttribute]
[DataMemberAttribute]
```

- In Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations, this creates a JSON-based custom service endpoint.
- In AX 2012, you expose the service through **AIF > Inbound ports** and deploy it from the AOT. AX 2012 does not provide the same native REST API style as Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations.

